= Agent self-identity — Technical =

== Persistent subject model ==
A persistent agent can be modeled as an enduring subject identifier <code>I</code> associated with time-varying state and authority:

<pre>
A_t = (I, S_t, G_t, C_t, P_t, H_t)
</pre>

where:

* <code>S_t</code> is mutable operational state;
* <code>G_t</code> is goals or commitments;
* <code>C_t</code> is configuration/constraints;
* <code>P_t</code> is permissions or delegated authority;
* <code>H_t</code> is provenance/history.

The decomposition is architecture-dependent. The important distinction is that the subject relation can persist while these components change.

== Identity resolution ==
A persistent-state access path can be represented schematically as:

<pre>
resolve subject I
→ resolve state namespace/version for I
→ authorize read
→ validate provenance/scope
→ reconstruct current state
→ act
→ write under resolved subject and scope
</pre>

Skipping subject resolution makes cross-agent state misbinding possible even when the stored records are individually valid.

== Identifier versus continuity ==
FIPA Agent Identifiers and modern workload identities provide ways to name or authenticate agents.

They do not by themselves solve persistence semantics. A continuity policy must still specify whether identity survives:

* credential rotation;
* process restart;
* host migration;
* model replacement;
* checkpoint restore;
* state fork.

== Fork semantics ==
A state clone creates a branching problem:

<pre>
copy(state) ≠ necessarily copy(identity)
</pre>

A migration may preserve identity; a fork may create child identities; a recovery operation may restore one identity from a checkpoint. The policy must define which interpretation applies.

== Security-principal boundary ==
IAM usually answers which principal is authenticated and what it may access.

Persistent self-identity can require an additional state-continuity relation:

<pre>
authenticated principal
+ persistent agent referent
+ state ownership
+ provenance
+ delegated authority
</pre>

These relations may coincide in a simple deployment and diverge in a compound agent system.

== BITwiki identity continuity invariant ==
The following is a BITwiki engineering synthesis:

<pre>
persistent read/write/action ⇒ resolved owning agent identity
</pre>

It should be treated as a design invariant, not a standardized theorem.

